# Architecture

`extension.js` constructs features, calls `enable()` in order, and `disable()` in reverse. Create a module when the first feature needs it. Do not create empty modules, and do not grow the template indicator.

```
extension.js       # lifecycle only
features/topBar.js # Main.panel
features/dock.js   # Main.overview.dash
lib/patch.js       # method replacements
lib/signals.js     # signal ids
```

A feature is a plain class: `constructor(extension)`, `enable()`, `disable()`. Features do not import or call each other. Look up `Main.panel` and `Main.overview` inside `enable()`, not in the constructor.

Push a feature before calling `enable()`. If `enable()` throws, the shell still calls `disable()`.

```javascript
enable() {
    this._features = [];

    for (const Feature of [TopBar, Dock]) {
        const feature = new Feature(this);
        this._features.push(feature);
        feature.enable();
    }
}

disable() {
    const features = this._features ?? [];

    for (let i = features.length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        features[i].disable();

    this._features = null;
}
```

Enable order is top bar, then dock. A third surface gets its own file under `features/`.

`features/dock.js` customizes `Main.overview.dash`. Do not build a persistent dock or integrate with Dash to Dock unless the task asks.

Add a `lib/` helper only when two features need it. Do not put feature behavior in `lib/`.

Leave the default user session. Do not add `unlock-dialog` or other `session-modes` unless the task says the feature must run on the lock screen.
